# {{ .prefix }}-subagent-driven-development

<!-- awf:section positioning -->
The subagent implementation chain iterates phases. It preserves sequential implementation dispatch and exclusive implementation-tool batching.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section per-task-review-note -->
**Phase review is report-only.** The parent settles findings after the phase-closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Invoke when a plan phase benefits from delegated implementation ownership. A plan may mix modes; hand inline phases to `{{ .prefix }}-executing-plans`.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Procedure
<!-- awf:section procedure-resolve-plan -->
1. Resolve the mutable plan and read every declared phase mode.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-raise-concerns -->
2. Stop for missing phase context, paths, boundaries, checks, or closing subject.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-extract-context -->
3. Per `{{ .layout.maintainableCodeDesign }}`, preserve the plan's settled structural choices, including a bounded enabling refactor; reassess them if grounded source contradicts them and stop and escalate rather than accept a bolt-on workaround. Extract the complete phase: goal, ordered tasks, exact paths, semantic boundary and ownership, external/internal representations and their translation point, allowed dependency direction, preparatory-refactor decision, prohibited bolt-on shortcuts, validation expectations, dependencies, prior phase commits, checks, closing subject, and V2 operation batch. The owner runs `awf context <the phase's exact paths>` before editing.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
The owner preserves the settled design or reports invalidating source facts; does not replan, broaden the task, or perform unrelated cleanup.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section dispatch-conventions -->
4. For a `subagent-driven` phase, verify a known clean and green baseline. Call one implementation child alone in its parent tool batch with `allowCommits: true` and provide the complete phase. {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Choose the smallest model expected to complete reliably: `small` is for narrow, mechanical, low-ambiguity work; `standard` is for substantive but bounded work; and `large` is for broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence work. Uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope requires reconsideration and possible escalation. Omit the `model` field entirely to use configured role routing; when the selected complexity warrants an override, pass the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value.{{ else }}Choose the smallest model expected to complete reliably: `small` is for narrow, mechanical, low-ambiguity work; `standard` is for substantive but bounded work; and `large` is for broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence work. Uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope requires reconsideration and possible escalation. Select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly; if this harness cannot select a model, use its default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable.{{ end }} The commit-capable owner performs all ordered work. Stage the complete transaction, run `awf check --staged`, then {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }}. Commit only after both commands pass; the hook repeats the staged check as defense in depth, then creates the declared phase-closing commit. Never dispatch concurrent implementers or helpers as phase owners.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-status-handling -->
5. Validate the reported commit and clean checkout, then dispatch one report-only phase review. {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Choose the smallest model expected to complete reliably: `small` is for narrow, mechanical, low-ambiguity work; `standard` is for substantive but bounded work; and `large` is for broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence work. Uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope requires reconsideration and possible escalation. Omit the `model` field entirely to use configured role routing; when the selected complexity warrants an override, pass the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value.{{ else }}Choose the smallest model expected to complete reliably: `small` is for narrow, mechanical, low-ambiguity work; `standard` is for substantive but bounded work; and `large` is for broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence work. Uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope requires reconsideration and possible escalation. Select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly; if this harness cannot select a model, use its default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable.{{ end }} The parent owns focused settlement commits and checkpoints only after findings resolve.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-routine -->

If an owner stops dirty, inventory `git status --short`, diff, completed and remaining work, prior concerns, and failed checks. Choose parent completion, restore the known green baseline and redispatch the complete revised phase, or stop for user input. An explicit transfer carries the complete revised phase, dirty-state inventory, completed and remaining work, prior concerns, and recovery verification; a blind successor instruction for one task is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section per-task-review -->
6. Review is report-only and phase-level; review fixes remain parent-owned and do not recreate the original transaction.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section final-task-adr-flip -->
7. Apply V2 batches atomically with matching claims and lifecycle events in the owning phase.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
8. After all settled phases, invoke `{{ .prefix }}-reviewing-impl`.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Notes
<!-- awf:section notes -->
- Sequential dispatch only, never parallel. Helpers remain sequential, commit-disabled, path-disjoint, and parent-integrated.
- Expected output is non-negotiable.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section red-flags -->
## Red flags

Do not casually dispatch over a dirty checkout, infer plan-wide mode, or let a helper own a shared file or closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
